A single StartProfile(frequency_hz=499) / 20s / StopProfile cycle over the MCP endpoint cost +1,387 MB RSS, and NONE of it was released after StopProfile returned.

Raw RSS series (20s samples), profiler window is unambiguous:
  t+0.3 .. t+3.7min   6748-6749 MB   flat, deltas within +/-1 MB
  t+4.0min            7786 MB        +1037  <- StartProfile
  t+4.3min            8136 MB        +350   <- still capturing
  t+4.7min            8136 MB        +0.1   <- StopProfile returned; NOT released
  +45s later          8136 MB        still retained

The returned folded-stack payload was only 2.3 MB, so the retention is internal (pprof sample buffers / symbol tables / backtrace storage), not the response.

WHY IT MATTERS: on the rack that is ~3% of a 48G cgroup permanently consumed per capture. The dangerous shape is someone running StartProfile on a box already near the limit IN ORDER TO DIAGNOSE why it is near the limit â€” which is exactly the situation we were in earlier tonight during the OOM crash loop. It is also a footgun for repeated captures: N profiles = N x ~1.4GB.

ASKS: free the profiler's buffers on StopProfile (or bound them), and until then a loud docstring on StartProfile stating the resident cost. Worth characterising across frequency_hz and duration â€” rship can re-measure on request; the harness is just RSS sampling around the two commands.

